      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a10.9.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g10.7.14', 'g10.9.4', 'g10.9.16'],
          lemma: "ἐγώ εἰμι ἡ θύρα",
          title: "“I am the door”",
          body: "Jesus turns the parable inward and takes a second role: not only the shepherd but the **door** itself. In a rough hillside fold the shepherd would sleep across the gap of the entrance — his own body *was* the gate; nothing came in or out except over him. So to enter “by me” is to **be saved**, and the saved sheep’s life is drawn in three homely verbs: it goes freely *in* to the shelter and *out* to graze, and it *finds pasture* — security and freedom and nourishment, all held in the one door. This is the fifth of John’s great **“I am”** sayings, each pairing the divine name (Exodus 3:14) with a picture of what Jesus gives: bread, light, and now the very gate of the fold.",
        },
      ],
