      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a10.28.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g10.28.4', 'g10.28.15', 'g10.30.5'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "“No one will snatch them out of my hand”",
          body: "Having said the sheep *hear, are known, and follow,* Jesus turns to what he *gives* them, and the promise is total. Eternal life is not a reward held out at the end but a gift placed in their hands *now* (“I give,” present tense); its consequence is stated with the strongest negative Greek allows — **“they shall never, ever perish.”** And their safety does not rest on the strength of their grip on him but on the strength of his grip on them: **“no one will snatch them out of my hand.”** Then he doubles the guarantee — the same sheep are also in the *Father’s* hand (v. 29), and no power is greater than the Father — before collapsing the two hands into one: **“I and the Father are one.”** This is the deep ground of Christian assurance. The sheep are not safe because they are strong, faithful, or clever; they are safe because they are *held,* by a shepherd whose hand is the Father’s hand, and out of that double grasp nothing in heaven or earth is able to tear them. It is worth noticing *what* he claims here: not merely that he loves the sheep, but that he possesses the divine power to keep them eternally — a thing only God can promise. The “oneness” of verse 30 is unity of power and will (and, John will insist, of being); it is precisely why the next verse has stones in hand.",
        },
      ],
