      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a10.34.1',
          kind: "grammar",
          refs: ['g10.34.15', 'g10.35.16', 'g10.36.16'],
          lemma: "θεοί ἐστε",
          title: "“You are gods” — the argument from Psalm 82",
          body: "Accused of blasphemy for calling himself God’s Son, Jesus reasons from their own Scripture (Psalm 82:6), where God addresses Israel’s appointed judges as **“gods”** — because “the word of God came to them,” i.e. they bore his authority and represented him. His logic runs from lesser to greater (a standard rabbinic move): *if Scripture — which “cannot be broken” — can call mere men “gods” simply because God’s word was entrusted to them, how is it blasphemy for the one whom the **Father consecrated and sent into the world** to call himself God’s Son?* The argument is defensive and disarming — it does not *reduce* his claim to the psalm’s level. He is not saying “I am a ‘god’ only in that borrowed sense”; he is exposing the inconsistency of a charge that would stone him for a word their own Law uses freely. The real weight of his identity rests not on Psalm 82 but on the two verbs of verse 36 — *consecrated and sent* — and on the “works of the Father” he keeps pointing them back to (v. 37–38): believe those, even if you will not believe him, and you will come to know that **“the Father is in me and I am in the Father.”**",
        },
      ],
